<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-matrices-crash-course" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Basics of Matrices and Vectors</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In this chapter we will learn how to solve <em>systems</em> of differential equations,
    where several unknown functions interact with each other. It turns out that a useful way to
    organize systems of ODEs is with <em>matrices</em>, so we start with a crash course.
    If you have seen matrices before, this page will be a quick review. If not, you'll need to spend some extra time 
    with the next few pages before moving on to the next section!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: MATRIX AND ENTRY NOTATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-matrix">
    <title>Matrix</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>matrix</term> is an <m>m \times n</m> array of numbers, with <m>m</m> rows
        and <m>n</m> columns. We can write <m>A = [a_{ij}]</m>, where <m>a_{ij}</m> is the
        entry in the <m>i</m>th row and <m>j</m>th column.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    When reading subscripts and sizes, notice the first index tells you the row, and the second tells you the column.
  </p>

  
    <p>
      Consider the matrix
      <me>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
      <m>A</m> has <m>2</m> rows and <m>3</m> columns, so <m>A</m> is a
      <m>2 \times 3</m> matrix.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the entries: <m>a_{11} = 1</m> is the entry in the first row, first column.
      The entry in the first row, second column is <m>a_{12} = 2</m>, and the entry in the
      second row, first column is <m>a_{21} = 0</m>. Rows first!
    </p>
  

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: SIZE AND ENTRY NOTATION (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-matrix-size-entry">
    <title>Size and Entry Practice</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the matrix
        <me>B = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 0 \\ 7 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        What is the size of <m>B</m> (in the form <m>m \times n</m>), and what is
        the entry <m>b_{32}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>B</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m>, and <m>b_{32} = 3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Rows first: <m>B</m> has <m>3</m> rows and <m>2</m> columns,
            and <m>b_{32}</m> is the entry in the third row, second column, which is <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>B</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m>, and <m>b_{32} = 7</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The size is right, but check the entry: <m>7</m> sits in the third row,
            <em>first</em> column, so it is <m>b_{31}</m>. The entry <m>b_{32}</m> is in
            the third row, second column.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>B</m> is <m>2 \times 3</m>, and <m>b_{32} = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful, rows come first in the size! <m>B</m> has <m>3</m> rows and
            <m>2</m> columns, so it is <m>3 \times 2</m>. And <m>b_{32}</m> means
            third row, second column.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>B</m> is <m>2 \times 3</m>, and <m>b_{32}</m> does not exist.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is what happens if the row and column counts get swapped. Rows first:
            <m>B</m> has <m>3</m> rows and <m>2</m> columns, so it is <m>3 \times 2</m>,
            and <m>b_{32}</m> (third row, second column) does exist.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MATRIX ADDITION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Let's quickly discuss <alert>matrix addition</alert>. Adding <m>A + B</m> works just as you'd expect it to: just add component-wise.
    Same with subtraction. The only requirement for addition is that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> must have the
    same dimensions (number of rows and columns) as each other.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-matrix-addition">
    <title>Adding Two Matrices</title>
    <p>
      Both matrices below are <m>2 \times 3</m>, so we may add them:
      <md>
        <mrow>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
          + \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
          \amp= \begin{bmatrix} -1+2 \amp 0+1 \amp 2+0 \\ 3+0 \amp 2+3 \amp 4-1 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 5 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
      </md>
      The result is again a <m>2 \times 3</m> matrix.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SCALAR MULTIPLICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now we will look at <alert>scalar multiplication</alert>, which is a product <m>cA</m>, where <m>c</m> is a
    <em>scalar</em> (meaning just a number, not a matrix). Computing <m>cA</m> also works as you might guess: just
    multiply <m>c</m> by each component of <m>A</m>.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-scalar-multiplication">
    <title>Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar</title>
    <p>
      With the scalar <m>c = 2</m> and the <m>3 \times 2</m> matrix below:
      <md>
        <mrow>2A = 2\begin{bmatrix} -2 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix}
          \amp= \begin{bmatrix} 2(-2) \amp 2 \cdot 3 \\ 2 \cdot 1 \amp 2 \cdot 0 \\ 2 \cdot 0 \amp 2 \cdot 5 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} -4 \amp 6 \\ 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 10 \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>

  <p>
   When we tie this back to differential equations, we will use functions as scalars, like <m>e^t</m>. For example,
    <me>e^t \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot e^t \\ 2 \cdot e^t \\ 3 \cdot e^t \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} e^t \\ 2e^t \\ 3e^t \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    Notice we can also write the scalar as the second factor:
    <me>\begin{bmatrix} e^t \\ 2e^t \\ 3e^t \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} e^t.</me>
    We will use this way of writing things a bunch when we solve systems later in the chapter!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VECTORS: COLUMN AND ROW
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>In addition to matrices, we will also need a good grasp on <em> vectors </em>. If you have seen them in physics
 or multivariate calculus, you may have been introduced to them as something with length and direction. In this course, 
we are mainly just considering them as lists of numbers (as we do in linear algebra). </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-vector">
    <title>Vectors</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>vector</term> is a finite list of numbers. It can be represented as an
        <m>n \times 1</m> matrix, called a <term>column vector</term>, or as a
        <m>1 \times n</m> matrix, called a <term>row vector</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
<p>There are several ways to express a vector. In this class, we will commonly write them as column vectors.</p>
  <example xml:id="ex-column-row-vectors">
    <title>Column and Row Vectors</title>
    <p>
      The column vector
      <me>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</me>
      is a <m>4 \times 1</m> matrix, so it has <m>4</m> components. Other common notations
      for this same vector are <m>(1, -1, 0, 4)</m> and <m>\langle 1, -1, 0, 4 \rangle</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The row vector
      <me>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</me>
      is a <m>1 \times 5</m> matrix, so it has <m>5</m> components.
    </p>
  </example>

